A||||3||RZURCTN||More timing questions!||||1||
Z||000000||RZURCTN||01-07-2000||02:27 PM||||While setting my timing on my 84 4 cyl.  today I had a little trouble.  When I point the timing light at the crank pulley the little notch doesn't exactly appear to stay in one place.  It keeps jumping back and forth a few degrees.  This made timing a little difficult to say the least.  Does that indicate a problem with my distributor? Or is it supposed to jump around?||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||01-07-2000||05:13 PM||||With the ALDL pins A and B connected together? It should be pretty solid. <P>However, then the pins are not shorted, it's not unusually to see it jump around. The ECM uses the timing to help smooth the idle. That's how GM can get at silky smooth idle.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ka4nkf||01-07-2000||05:27 PM||||If you have shorted out the ECM connector and it still jumps around, then you have a worn camshaft gear or your dist. has a badly worn gear. If everything is top notch the timming mark will stay steady.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RZURCTN||01-07-2000||06:03 PM||||I shorted the ALDL, ALCL, or the diagnostic terminal(whatever you prefer to call it!) when I timed it.  With it shorted it jumps around a little bit.  I still haven't figured out the problem with my EGR valve yet either, so I don't know if this has anything to do with it.  That's a topic for another post!||||reg||1
